<Project>
  <!-- Every production assembly exposes its internals to its own unit test assembly, which is named
       after it with a .tests.unit suffix. This keeps the public API surface limited to what callers
       are meant to use while still letting the tests exercise internal members directly. Test
       projects are skipped because they have no unit test assembly of their own.

       This belongs in Directory.Build.targets rather than Directory.Build.props because both
       $(AssemblyName) and $(IsTestProject) are only final once the project body has been evaluated.
       Directory.Build.props is imported before that, so a project setting its own <AssemblyName>
       would be pointed at the default name instead of the one it chose. -->
  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(IsTestProject)' != 'true'">
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="$(AssemblyName).tests.unit" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- Package validation, for projects that ship. This is here rather than in
       Directory.Build.props for the same reason as the item above: $(IsPackable) is only final once
       the project body has been evaluated, so a condition in .props reads the inherited default
       instead of the project's own choice — and silently enabled validation on the test project,
       which does not ship, when this lived there.

       No readme, icon or other content is packed from here on purpose. What goes INTO a package
       describes that package, so the project that ships declares it, alongside the matching
       $(PackageReadmeFile) / $(PackageIcon). A shared item here would put the repository README into
       every package in the solution regardless of whether it says anything about them. -->
  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(IsPackable)' == 'true'">
    <EnablePackageValidation>true</EnablePackageValidation>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!-- Two assembly-level declarations that the strict rule set asks every assembly for, satisfying
       CA1014/S3990 (CLSCompliant) and CA1017/S3992 (ComVisible). They are emitted here rather than
       written into an AssemblyInfo.cs per project so that a new project inherits them.

       ComVisible(false) is the safe default for managed code: it keeps types out of the COM type
       library unless a type opts in, which is what almost every .NET assembly wants.

       CLSCompliant(true) is a promise about the PUBLIC surface — that a consumer written in any .NET
       language can use it — so the compiler starts checking the public API against the CLS rules
       (CS3001 and friends) once it is set. That promise is only meaningful for an assembly someone
       else consumes, and only testable against a public surface, so it is scoped to projects that
       ship. A test project or an app host has no external consumer and declaring it there would
       assert something nobody can rely on. $(IsPackable) is final by the time this file is imported,
       which is why this lives in .targets and not .props. -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- _Parameter1_IsLiteral keeps the value unquoted in the generated AssemblyInfo.cs. Without it
         MSBuild writes ComVisible("false") and the compiler rejects it with CS1503. -->
    <AssemblyAttribute Include="System.Runtime.InteropServices.ComVisible">
      <_Parameter1>false</_Parameter1>
      <_Parameter1_IsLiteral>true</_Parameter1_IsLiteral>
    </AssemblyAttribute>
    <AssemblyAttribute Include="System.CLSCompliant" Condition="'$(IsPackable)' == 'true'">
      <_Parameter1>true</_Parameter1>
      <_Parameter1_IsLiteral>true</_Parameter1_IsLiteral>
    </AssemblyAttribute>
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- The other half of the CLSCompliant decision above. CA1014 and S3990 ask every assembly for the
       attribute, and they report it against the assembly rather than against a file — the diagnostic
       comes out as `CSC : warning CA1014` with no path — so an .editorconfig section cannot reach
       them however it is scoped. $(NoWarn) is the only lever, and it carries the same $(IsPackable)
       guard as the attribute so the two cannot drift apart: an assembly either makes the promise or
       is not asked for it.

       CS1591, the missing-XML-doc warning, rides along on the same guard for a related reason.
       $(GenerateDocumentationFile) is true for EVERY project in Directory.Build.props, and not
       because anything reads the .xml: IDE0005 (remove unnecessary usings) cannot run without it,
       because the unused-import analysis is a by-product of the doc-comment binding pass, and
       Roslyn reports that it skipped the rule as the `EnableGenerateDocumentationFile` advisory —
       which $(TreatWarningsAsErrors) turns into a Release error. Nothing else in the analyzer set
       catches an unused using; SonarAnalyzer's S1128 is one of the rules it ships disabled, so
       turning the doc file off trades the error for silence. So the file is always generated, and
       the demand for a comment on every public member applies only where there is a consumer to
       read it. See https://github.com/dotnet/roslyn/issues/41640. -->
  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(IsPackable)' != 'true'">
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CA1014;S3990;CS1591</NoWarn>
  </PropertyGroup>
</Project>
